2.32.m:
Vyāsa said:
A drinker of liquor should himself drink heated liquor burning like fire;
when his body is consumed by it, that best of the twice-born is freed.

Having killed a brāhmaṇa woman, a vipra should observe the vow for eight years;
for a kṣatriya woman, six years; for a vaiśya woman, three years;
and having slain a śūdra woman, the best of the twice-born is purified in one year.

Having killed a Vaiśya woman inadvertently, one should give something to a twice-born.
And in the case of killing outcasts, one should perform the cāndrāyaṇa observance;
or purification comes by the parāka—so said the unborn lord.

Having slain a dog, he should drink milk for three nights, fully self-controlled,
or for a cat or a mongoose walk a yojana along the road;
and for the slaying of a horse, a twice-born should perform the twelve-night kṛcchra penance.

For the slaying of elephants, the taptakṛcchra penance is prescribed as the purification;
and having killed a cow inadvertently, the cāndrāyaṇa or parāka penance;
but for the intentional slaying of a cow, no expiation is found.

Thus ends the thirty-second chapter in the latter section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.

2.33.m:
Having eaten human flesh, he should perform the cāndrāyaṇa;
and having eaten a crow, a dog, an elephant,
a boar, or a domestic fowl, he is purified by the taptakṛcchra.

And for [eating] the meat of carnivorous animals, or the feces or urine
of cows, jackals, and monkeys, he should perform the same vow;
and having fasted for twelve days, he should offer ghee with the kūṣmāṇḍa mantras.

Having eaten a mongoose, an owl, or a cat, he should perform the sāntapana;
having eaten carnivorous beasts, camels, or donkeys, he is purified by the taptakṛcchra;
and his initiatory rite [must be performed] like a vow, according to the earlier rule.

2.35.m:
Then, seeing Rudra, Maheśa, the sole lord of all, coming not far away
with the Goddess of the gods, his fear departed;
[meanwhile] Time approached to drag off that royal seer.
